lture and community organizations  including the New York State vegetable  Growers Association he was vice  president and as well with New York Farm  Bureau Board of Directors a member of  executive committee member of audit  committee chairman of Labor Committee  now he has served as the Commissioner of  New York State Department of Agriculture  and markets since 2014 and I've been  truly honored to partner with him on  critically important initiatives that  he's launched from the newly  reinvigorated Council on hunger and food  policy to The Milk Marketing Advisory  Council and we collectively strive to  ensure that the Commissioner can count  on all of our SUNY schools to step up to  the plate to assist the state and our  agricultural communities please join me  in welcoming Commissioner Richard ball  that was a nice way of saying you got an  overdressed farmer in front of you right  really great to be here it's great to  see the crowd has gotten kind of  energized a little bigger there's a lot  of interest in this subject and thank  you so much Dean we had an awesome  discussion this morning we had the  Lieutenant Governor here and some of  your elected representatives and some of  them still here but we had a great  discussion a great summary we looked at  where the industry is now we looked at  what we've done so far we looked at our  potential and we looked at where the  opportunities for the state to intersect  and help grow it and I think we're  pretty much all on the same page and to  kind of summarize that I won't talk  anymore but I would direct your  attention once again to the to the  screen we showed a video this morning  we're going to show it again because we  have a number of new people and because  we like the video so if you would direct  your attention to the thousands of  years-- industrial hemp has been a  valuable and versatile crop once a top  commodity on American farms production  was driven out by other emerging  industries and eventually the federal  government made it illegal to grow until  now recognizing its vast potential the  federal farm bill allowed States to  launch pilot programs for industrial  hemp research and in 2015 Governor Cuomo  announced a bold plan to put the Empire  State at the forefront of the country's  hemp provided with the type of  legislative action that Governor Cuomo  has led with with Albany  it's making a real difference partnering  with SUNY Morrisville JD farms in  Madison County harvested New York's  first industrial hemp crop in decades  Cornell University wasn't far behind it  was made possible through Governor  Cuomo's industrial hemp research pilot  program allowing up to ten permits to  grow and research hemp as an  agricultural commodity study nitrogen  content Mesilla we studied seeding rates  New York is abundant in water abundant  and arable land and for us this crop  made a lot of sense  hemp research is now expanding across  the state I think hemp is not only a  great opportunity for diversification on  the ground but it could lead to a lot of  great job and manufacturing  opportunities that we've lost sort of  today to globalization hemp and  marijuana may look similar but make no  mistake these plants have a significant  difference I think the major thing for  people to realize is that industrial  hemp is not marijuana even though it  looks like it THC is the mind-altering  drug and in marijuana and industrial  half have very very low levels of that  generations of innovators have pioneered  new markets for this dynamic crop using  every part of the plant to drive growth  in vital industries and boost the  profitability of the agricultural sector  today the US retail market for  industrial hemp is nearly six hundred  million dollars and makes a good  insulation as well as wallboard for  buildings will be useful for automobiles  pressboard and furniture bags and  biodegradable packaging material so  shampoos cosmetic products you shell  hemp seeds themselves or tasty and  they're a great superfood now with the  support of the state legislature  Governor Cuomo is taking the program to  the next level removing the cap on the  number of permits for hemp research  supporting unprecedented exploration of  the crops viability and encouraging New  York's best and brightest to discover  and rediscover hemp potential and as we  have more opportunity to do research I  think we can just make it a better crop  and a more economical crop across the  board we could bring lots of industry  back to New York State put a lot of  people back to work  Shep is really an industrial crop it  should just regain its rightful place in  the American agricultural landscape the  global demand for industrial hemp is  growing and here in New York the  possibilities are endless possibilities  are endless you know one of the words  that the Dean and I'd like to share with  each other quite a bit is collaboration  we talked about that a little bit  earlier but I think when we collaborate  and we have our partners here in the  room and we have our partners on the  same page who can be against this I like  our I like our chances here  the governor is very much committed to  this idea he's excited about hemp and we  talked about in our regular basis so  this is I just want to say from the  state standpoint we're committed to  staying with this and doing the things  that we need to do so there's a vision  behind all of this  there's a very practical side of it  today's summit is even though we've been  working at this many of you for a long  time certainly many of us at the state  level for a couple of years now coming  up with the ideas and doing the  necessary work to get it over the hump  we're not done yet there's more to do in  this summit is really just the beginning  so excited to be here the Lieutenant  Governor when we got done with our  session this morning we had a good  conversation with a number of our  partners we heard some needs  we heard some things that needed to to  be done in order of us for us to take  those next steps going forward and I'm  just going to summarize for you now some  of the deliverables from this morning  session that are underway as you know  the first legislation which was  sponsored by my colleague here to the  left it helped us get started and  allowed the Department  to designate some areas attached to  research institutions to begin to do  some work on hemp in New York State  well we've issued the first ever  research permits to private farms and  businesses and previously that pilot  program was limited to the research  institutions but at the governor's  direction we've expanded the pilot to  include private entities so six of those  private farms and businesses were issued  permits bring the total the total number  of hemp research sites across the state  to ten so we've added 21st century hemp  and our RIT in Rochester we've added  Cavallaro farms in Goshen New York once  again JD farms in eat in New York old  Mud Creek farm in Hudson New York the  plant science labs in Buffalo and  pre-process in ellsberg New York a  couple of other things of note that we  heard from our partners this morning  going forward we're going to seek DEA  permit allowing businesses to import  seed and establish a seed distribution  site for international imports we heard  about the importance of C importing seed  is central to research in New York State  and in addition to that we are seeking a  US Drug Enforcement Agency permit to  import the seed so the New York State  Department of Ag and markets will  advocate to ease the federal  requirements to acquire hemp seed so  that will be making the federal efforts  as well but in the meantime we will hold  the permit at the Department happy to  announce that we're going to award  $400,000 to Cornell University to  continue to expand the research we heard  that loud and clear  the researchers at Cornell's AG  Experiment Station in Geneva are 