ges to minimize the  oversight of the research done by the  educational institutions as it's  appropriate and I think this is clearly  very important point to make here  advocate for the federal policies to  boost the industry we're still  restricted we still have this cloud  hanging over our head and over this  industry but the governor has an office  in Washington DC they're very active  they're on the same page with you and  with us they have influence with the  Congress down there for me at the  Department of Agriculture and at our  other agencies in the state we all have  national relationships with other  commissioners secretaries and directors  and that organization will also advocate  and we'll work through those channels as  well to help advance what we know to be  the right direction to go in so I think  I'm encouraged I'm encouraged at the  national level I'm encouraged certainly  here at the state level I think as you  go through this afternoon you will see  the quality of program the quality of  people involved in here and you should  be encouraged as well with that let me  introduce our partner and Assemblymember  downlo Pardo who has sent me more emails  in the last year than I've got from any  other single source I think she's very  focused she's very passionate she cares  a great deal about her people her farms  her agriculture here in New York State  and this mission so with that thank you  she's never given me that email address  that was your first problem well hi  everyone it's really very nice of you to  all be here today and you share I'm sure  aren't interested passion in this new  exciting crop industrial hemp you know  many of us I'm sure in the room are old  enough to have you know lamented for a  very very long time that this crop was  something we could not grow and as I  became more familiar with the potential  of this crop it was really unacceptable  to be honest with you that we had a  consumer demand for products people were  purchasing products people were  manufacturing products with imported  materials and it didn't make any sense  to me whatsoever that we weren't able to  afford our farmers that opportunity and  to give manufacturers homegrown local  product and when the farm bill came  along which allowed New York State to  become a place where we could do pilot  research senator O'Mara and I jumped all  over it and I'm you know it was a bit of  a stretch to people we had to go one by  one with the vote ham why industrial  hemp sheet and explained to people right  off the bat that it was not marijuana it  was a cousin it was this it was that and  they finally understood especially for  places like upstate New York the  enormous economic potential for not only  farmers but for manufacturers I come  from Endicott New York and represent  Endicott and Binghamton area which was  all about shoes and computers it was a  manufacturing town so many places are as  well and to get the economic developers  to have this come to clarity for them  that this isn't just about growing a  crop this is about producing goods and  services but making goods again in New  York State and I think that's what  really began to turn people around about  this the following year the farm bill  gave us another opportunity to not only  research this crop but to also market it  transport it process it and sell it if  it were not for that particular bill all  the research crops would have had to  have been destroyed which would be a  terrible thing because the forty acres  of beautiful hemp that you're seeing and  depicted in this logo behind us that JD  farms was just a spectacular thing to  see and smells pretty good too by the  way let's say and so we are now able to  to take advantage full advantage of what  they have allowed we've stocked this at  the federal level whatever they've  allowed we've come right behind it  Senator Rand Paul and Mitch McConnell  are the sponsors of these bills and some  of our own senator senator Gillibrand  are sponsors of the industrial hemp  farming act you know while they are  trying to figure this out in Washington  New York State is positioning itself to  be very competitive an all manner of  hemp whether it's in the pharmaceutical  nutraceutical textiles of various food  and other other products that are  already in the works it's very exciting  so when the federal government does  relax this which will make it a little  bit easier for us we will have the  Northeast pretty much sewed up as far as  I can tell and all of you who are in the  room who expecting to get involved in  this  I think will be very pleased with  outcome commissioner a ball mentioned  some of the fabulous developments at the  state level we're very lucky that the  state is willing to really come to  assist on the de a seed certification a  seed acquisition issue and all the rest  of these considerations with research  dollars becoming behind it and trying to  make it easier for those of you who have  been involved in the craft beverage  industry once the state got its head  wrapped around making it easier for  craft beverages you have seen the  results and I can tell you without a  shadow of a doubt that this is the next  thing that the governor and team are  putting their full force and focus  behind it's truly a very exciting time  for those of us who've long long wish  that Hamm could be receiving its due so  what I want to just say in closing is  that just to get everybody on the same  page we had 10 research licenses as part  of the original program and those have  all been filled what is about to happen  now as part of the recent budget is  going again the Commissioner mentioned  they're going to be unlimited licenses  available but regs have to be  established for that so we're a little  we're a little behind for this growing  season but I would fully expect and I  hope correct me if I'm wrong the 2018  will very likely be the year when we can  see a much larger build-out among  farmers who are interested it's going to  be critical however that those farmers  understand the supply chain and are  going to be growing for a particular  market so they're not just randomly  growing some random cultivar they're  going to be growing from a particular  market and that market is going to have  processing available and we have in the  mix proposals right now to do hubs in  the area of whether it be on CBD oil or  in fiber so in 2018 you may very well be  in a position to have a marriage with a  particular company that wants a certain  type of strain and you may have a  processor in the mix so it's all  seamless with the assistance from the  state  so thanks for your patience over these  years and decades for some of us who  have cared about this plant our time has  finally come and I'm absolutely  delighted to be part of it and happy to  to play whatever role I need to in the  future thank you this has been a  production of Cornell University on the  web at cornell edu

